*** LIST-INTERFACE ***

✅ Maintains insertion order (Elements stay in the order they were added).
✅ Allows duplicate elements (Unlike Set<T>, which only keeps unique elements).
✅ Supports index-based access (get(index), set(index, value)).
✅ Provides methods for searching and sublist (indexOf(), lastIndexOf(), subList()).

java.lang.Iterable<T> --- Interface
   ├── java.util.Collection<T> ----Interface
   │      ├── java.util.List<T>   (Ordered, allows duplicates) ---Interface
   │           ├── ArrayList<T>   (Fast random access, resizable array)-----Class
   │           ├── LinkedList<T>  (Doubly linked list, fast insertions/deletions)-----Class
   │           ├── Vector<T>      (Thread-safe ArrayList)-----Class
   │           ├── Stack<T>       (LIFO - Last In, First Out)



* Key Points for Interviews

✅ List<T> maintains insertion order and allows duplicates.
✅ Supports index-based operations (get(), set(), indexOf()).
✅ ArrayList<T> is faster for retrieval, while LinkedList<T> is better for frequent insertions/deletions.
✅ Vector<T> is synchronized, making it thread-safe but slower.
✅ Stack<T> follows LIFO (Last In, First Out) behavior.


