* ARRAY-LIST :-

 ✅ Maintains insertion order (Elements remain in the order they were added).
 ✅ Allows duplicate elements (Unlike Set<T>, which removes duplicates).
    Allows Null as well.
 ✅ Provides fast access (O(1)) via get(index).
 ✅ Automatically resizes when more elements are added.
 ✅ Slower insert/delete (O(n)) because shifting is required.


2. ArrayList<T> Class Hierarchy

  java.lang.Iterable<T>
     ├── java.util.Collection<T>
     │      ├── java.util.List<T>
     │           ├── java.util.ArrayList<T>  (Resizable array implementation)



3. Methods in ArrayList<T>


*  add(T element)	                :       Adds an element at the end.
*  add(int index, T element)	    :       Adds an element at a specific index.
*  get(int index)	                :       Retrieves an element at a given index.
*  set(int index, T element)	    :       Updates an element at a given index.
*  remove(Object o)	                :       Removes the first occurrence of an element.
*  remove(int index)	            :       Removes the element at the given index.
*  subList(int fromIndex, int toIndex)	    Returns a sublist.
*  clear()	                        :       Removes all elements.
*  isEmpty()	                    :       Checks if the list is empty.



4. Key Points for Interviews

✅ ArrayList<T> is a resizable array (size grows automatically).
✅ Best for fast retrieval (O(1)) but slow for insertions/removals (O(n)).
✅ Allows duplicate elements and maintains insertion order.
✅ Not thread-safe (Use Collections.synchronizedList() for multi-threading).
✅ By default, initial capacity is 10, and it grows by 1.5 times when full.


