* Linked-List :-

-  LinkedList is the doubly linked list implementation of list and dequeue.
-  It allows efficient insertion and deletion of elements.
-  slow random access compare to ArrayList.
-  it implements both List and Queue Behaviour. Meaning both LIFO and FIFO are Possible.
-  Stores non-contiguous memory (each element is a separate node).
-  Slower access (O(n)) compared to ArrayList (O(1)).

java.lang.Iterable<T>
   └── java.util.Collection<T>
        └── java.util.List<T>
             └── java.util.LinkedList<T>  (Doubly Linked List)



Feature	                        ArrayList (Dynamic Array)	        LinkedList (Doubly Linked List)

Insertion Order	                ✅ Maintains order	                   ✅ Maintains order

Duplicates Allowed	            ✅ Yes	                               ✅ Yes

Null Allowed	                ✅ Yes	                               ✅ Yes

Random Access (get(index))	    🔥 Fast (O(1))	                       ❌ Slow (O(n))

Insertion/Deletion (Middle)	    ❌ Slow (O(n))	                       🔥 Fast (O(1))

Insertion/Deletion (Start/End)	❌ Slow (O(n) at start, O(1) at end)   🔥 Fast (O(1))

Memory Usage	                ✅ Less	                               ❌ More (extra pointers)




Example :-

import java.util.LinkedList;

public class LinkedListExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedList<String> list = new LinkedList<String>();
        list.add("Anand");
        list.add("Kulkarni");

        list.forEach(print -> System.out.println(print));
    }
}



