* Vector :-


- Vector is dynamic array that will grow and shrink dynamically when element added and removed.
- Vector is synchronized, Meaning it is thread safe unlike arraylist.

🔹 Key Features of Vector
✅ Allows duplicates
✅ Allows Null Values
✅ Maintains insertion order
✅ Thread-safe (synchronized)
✅ Resizes dynamically (default capacity doubles when full)


java.lang.Iterable
   └── java.util.Collection
         └── java.util.List
               └── java.util.Vector


🔹 Vector vs ArrayList
Feature	               Vector	                        ArrayList
Thread-safety	      ✅ Yes (Synchronized)	           ❌ No (Not Synchronized)
Performance	          ⬇ Slower (Locks entire list)	   ⬆ Faster (No locks)
Growth	              Doubles capacity when full	    Increases by 50%
Legacy?	              ✅ Yes (Since Java 1.0)	        ✅ No (Introduced in Java 1.2)



Example :-

import java.util.Vector;

public class VectorExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vector<String> vector = new Vector<>();

        // Adding elements
        vector.add("Anand");
        vector.add("Kulkarni");
        vector.add("Anagha");

        // Accessing elements
        System.out.println("First element: " + vector.firstElement());
        System.out.println("Last element: " + vector.lastElement());

        // Iterating using forEach
        vector.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}

